<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Breitenfeld (1631)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1631">1631</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Breitenfeld · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Breitenfeld was fought in 1631 near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a> during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>. The Swedish king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustavus-adolphus">Gustavus Adolphus</a>, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> allies, decisively defeated the Catholic imperial army under Tilly. The victory demonstrated the effectiveness of Swedish military reforms and established the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swedish-empire">Swedish Empire</a> as a major European power.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-breitenfeld-1631.jpg" alt="Battle of Breitenfeld (1631)" width="320" height="208" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: DCHNwam · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:29a_Gustave_Adolphe_%C3%A0_la_bataille_de_Breitenfeld,_1632.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Swedish intervention in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a> began in June 1630, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustavus-adolphus">Gustavus Adolphus</a> landed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a> with nearly 18,000 troops. Hopes of widespread German support largely failed, and by the end of 1630 his only new ally was Christian William, whose capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a> was besieged by Tilly. The Sack of Magdeburg in May 1631 warned the German princes of the consequences of an Imperial victory. The 1631 Treaty of Bärwalde, provided funds for the Swedes and their Protestant allies, and Gustavus raised his forces to around 36,000. He allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-george-i-elector-of-saxony">John George I, Elector of Saxony</a>, and the combined army marched on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a>, where Tilly was based.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The battle began at noon on 17 September 1631 with a cannonade of about two hours. The Swedes fired three to five volleys to each Imperial one, and Tilly's tightly packed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tercio">tercios</a> made easy targets for Torstenson's guns. At two o'clock Pappenheim charged the Swedish right wing on his own initiative, but Swedish musketeers placed among the cavalry broke each attempt. Imperial cavalry meanwhile routed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxon</a> centre and left, and the Saxons fled.</p><p>Tilly then turned his infantry against the Swedish left under Horn. Dust and the wind disordered the manoeuvre, and Horn bent his line back and held on, with Colonel Caldenbach's regiment sacrificed to delay the attackers. At about five o'clock <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustavus-adolphus">Gustavus Adolphus</a> led a cavalry charge up the Galgenberg, seized the Imperial guns and turned them on Tilly's infantry, while other Swedish brigades cut off its retreat towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leipzig">Leipzig</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Imperial and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholic-league">Catholic League</a> losses were between 7,000 and 8,000 killed, with around 6,000 captured, and Tilly lost his artillery, his war chest and 120 banners, which were sent to the Riddarholmen Church in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stockholm">Stockholm</a>. The Swedes lost 3,550 men and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> between 2,000 and 3,000. Captured Imperial soldiers were soon recruited into the Swedish ranks.</p><p>It was the first major Catholic defeat in a war then 13 years old, and it sent shockwaves around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. A council of war at Halle weighed a direct march on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>, but the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> were sent towards the Austrian Hereditary Lands and Vienna while the Swedes moved south-west towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>. Historians have long questioned that choice, though it cannot be known whether a march on Vienna would have brought favourable terms in 1632. The Swedish brigade of mobile battalions had also shown its advantage over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tercio">tercio</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Breitenfeld (1631)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Thirty Years’ War John George I first stayed neutral, then joined Gustavus Adolphus of Sweden; the Saxon contingent fled at the <b>Battle of Breitenfeld</b> in 1631, and Saxony suffered heavily from both sides before its separate Peace of Prague with the emperor in 1635.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electorate-of-saxony">Electorate of Saxony · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>He won Ingria from Russia by the Treaty of Stolbovo in 1617, took Riga in 1621 and in 1630 entered the Thirty Years’ War, winning the <b>Battle of Breitenfeld</b> in 1631 before he was killed at Lützen in 1632.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sweden">Kingdom of Sweden · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>At <b>Breitenfeld</b> (1631), Swedish and Saxon forces routed Tilly's veteran Catholic League army, and at Lützen (1632) the Swedes held the field against Wallenstein even though Gustavus himself was killed.</blockquote><a href="/empire/swedish-empire">Swedish Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>…the Thirty Years War, Gustavus Adolphus of Sweden combined drilled musketeers, light field artillery, and disciplined cavalry into a system that won a great victory at <b>Breitenfeld</b> in 1631 and made a poor and thinly peopled northern kingdom, for a time, one of the great powers of Europe.</blockquote><a href="/essays/the-military-revolution">The Military Revolution</a></li></ul></section>
